<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Limburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1065 CE – 1795 CE (approx.) · capital Limbourg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small duchy in the hills east of the Meuse whose succession war ended at the battle of Worringen in 1288, binding it to Brabant for five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbourg">Limbourg</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vesdre">Vesdre</a> was built in the early 11th century, and around 1065 Walram Udo of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arlon">Arlon</a> became count of Limburg. His son Henry I claimed the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a>, and later rulers kept the ducal title for their own lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Limburg was a land of pasture, woods and later cloth and lead mining around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verviers">Verviers</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vesdre">Vesdre</a> valley. Its dukes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waleran-iii-duke-of-limburg">Waleran III</a>, intermarried with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> nobility. When the line ended in 1283, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-duke-of-brabant">John I of Brabant</a> fought for the inheritance and won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Worringen</a> in 1288.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Limburg was then ruled with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant</a>, passing to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a>. In 1661 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-overmaas">Overmaas</a> lands were divided with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the rest in 1795.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-limburg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Limburg" width="320" height="376"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Duke_of_Limburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1065 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Limbourg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry I of Limburg, Waleran III of Limburg, John I of Brabant</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French and Limburgish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Limburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=duchy-of-limburg" aria-label="Duchy of Limburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4815 420 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Limburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5135 676L5229 694M5135 676L5098 671"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5098 671h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5135 676h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5135 676h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Limbourg. <a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=duchy-of-limburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

